LAB2 is not an issue nowadays as that's an exam and it doesn't contain the 
diagnostic test as it used too.
So we will not send reminders about that, but we will send reminders about the 
other three modules.
Those tree modules can also be finished at any time, unlike LAB2 that must be 
taken when the exam is given.

Here we provide a script that will email students at suitable points in time 
and intervals.

\subsection{The script}

The script is a bash script.
Cron will execute the script at suitable points in time.
We also want to be able to source it for debugging and use in other scripts.
<<[[missing.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash

<<constants>>
<<functions>>

# Run the script if not sourced
if [[ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" == "${0}" ]]; then
  main
fi
@

\subsection{When to run the script}

The course is given during autumns, September to mid-January.
The different modules have different timelines.

KAL1 should be done by October.
The labs can usually be presented at most lab sessions during the course.
After the course ends it can only be presented during Lab Week in June.

LAB1 should be done by November.
These labs can also be presented at any lab session during the course.
After the course ends they can be presented at Restlabb every week during the 
semester, but also during Lab Week in June.

LAB3 should be done by mid-January.
After the course ends they can present these assignments at Restlabb every week 
during the semester, but also during Lab Week in June.
The diagnostic test can be done at any time as it requires no grading.

The script should optimally be run around the time the students think that they 
are done with a module.
Then I want a reminder at the end of every month.
(This ought to be researched.
Particularly, it should be founded in the nudging research literature.)

Cron will run the script at the end of every month.
So the [[main]] function must look at the current month and determine which 
modules to check for.

\subsection{The email}

We get the students' email addresses as the third field of the output of 
[[canvaslms results --missing]].
(The field that we [[cut]] away above.)
We want to send one email per student.
We can use the [[mutt]] command to send the email.

We want the subject to contain the course (column 1) and something indicating 
that this is an automatic reminder about things left to do to get a grade.

The body of the email should contain a list of the missing assignments and the 
motivations for why they are missing.
It should also contain a brief description of how to proceed.
For example, for the missing diagnostic tests, the email should say that they 
should just do it and then they'll get the grade.
But for other assignments, they might need to present during a lab session or 
at Restlabb, then this should be mentioned in the body of the email.


\section{Configuration variables}

The script takes the course identifier as its first positional argument,
following the same convention as [[datorprov.sh]].
This makes the script reusable across course instances (e.g.\ [[prgi25]],
[[prgi26]]) without editing, and lets cron invoke it as
\mintinline{bash}{./missing.sh prgi25}.
<<constants>>=
course="$1"
shift
@

The four module names mirror the Ladok modules and the Canvas assignment
groups.
We define them as constants so that a name change in Canvas only requires
updating one place.
We also name the summary strategy here because it must be passed to every
[[canvaslms results --missing]] call; defining it once prevents the calls
from drifting out of sync.
<<constants>>=
MODULE_KAL1="KAL1"
MODULE_LAB1="LAB1"
MODULE_LAB3="LAB3"
SUMMARY_STRATEGY="canvaslms.grades.conjunctavgsurvey"
@

The diagnostic test is the one LAB3 assignment that students can complete
entirely on their own at any time --- it requires no presentation and no
grader.
Naming it here lets [[how_to_proceed]] give specific guidance for it
without embedding the Swedish assignment title in the logic.
The Restlabb URL is the drop-in session where students present all other
assignments after the course ends.
<<constants>>=
DIAGNOSTIC_TEST="Avslutande diagnostiskt prov"
RESTLABB_URL="https://www.kth.se/social/group/labbvecka/page/restlabbar-7/"
@


\section{Dry-run support}

Testing a script that sends email is risky: a single off-by-one in the
student loop would flood real inboxes.
We therefore support a [[DRY_RUN]] mode in which [[run_or_echo]] prints the
command it would execute instead of running it.
Setting [[DRY_RUN=1]] in the environment is enough; the default is live
mode.
<<functions>>=
run_or_echo() {
  if [ "${DRY_RUN:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "$@"
  else
    "$@"
  fi
}
@


\section{Overview of the implementation}

Before diving into each piece, here is the complete function dependency
graph.
It shows how [[main]] orchestrates the reminder flow and which helpers each
function relies on.

\begin{verbatim}
main
 └─ send_reminders(modules...)
     ├─ missing_for_module(module)   [called once per active module]
     ├─ email_subject()
     └─ email_body(student, all_missing)
         ├─ format_module_rows(module, student_rows)  [once per module]
         ├─ module_instructions(module)               [once per module]
         │   [uses: MODULE_KAL1, LAB_WEEK_URL]
         └─ how_to_proceed(student_rows)
             [uses: DIAGNOSTIC_TEST, MODULE_KAL1, MODULE_LAB3,
                    RESTLABB_URL]

run_or_echo   [used inside send_reminders when invoking mutt]
\end{verbatim}

The key design choice is that [[send_reminders]] collects all missing
rows from Canvas in one pass (one [[canvaslms]] call per active module)
and then distributes that data to per-student email composition.
This avoids re-querying Canvas once per student, which would be
prohibitively slow.


\section{The main function}

[[main]] looks at the current calendar month and decides which modules
have passed their deadlines, then delegates to [[send_reminders]].

We want reminders to start \emph{after} each module's due date, not
before, so that students are not nagged about work that is not yet due.
The course runs from September to mid-January, so the dispatch table is 
\cref{tab:dispatch}.

\begin{table}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrl}
\toprule
Month & Numeric & Active modules\\
\midrule
September & 9 & KAL1\\
October & 10 & KAL1, LAB1\\
November & 11 & KAL1, LAB1\\
December & 12 & KAL1, LAB1\\
January--May & 1--5 & KAL1, LAB1, LAB3\\
June--August & 6--8 & \emph{(none)}\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\caption{Active modules by month.}
\label{tab:dispatch}
\end{table}

The year wraps between December (12) and January (1), so a simple
\(month \ge 9\) test does not cover spring months.
We handle the wrap by checking either the autumn condition or the spring
condition: a month is \enquote{active for KAL1} if it is \(\ge 9\)
(autumn) \emph{or} \(\le 5\) (spring semester, where Restlabb still runs).
The [[10#]] prefix forces decimal interpretation so that months like
[[09]] are not mis-read as octal.
<<functions>>=
main() {
  local month
  month=$(date +%m)
  local modules=()
  <<add KAL1 if month is in range>>
  <<add LAB1 if month is in range>>
  <<add LAB3 if month is in range>>
  send_reminders "${modules[@]}"
}
@

<<add KAL1 if month is in range>>=
if [[ 10#$month -ge 9 || 10#$month -le 5 ]]; then
  modules+=("$MODULE_KAL1")
fi
@

LAB1 reminders start one month later than KAL1, in October.
<<add LAB1 if month is in range>>=
if [[ 10#$month -ge 10 || 10#$month -le 5 ]]; then
  modules+=("$MODULE_LAB1")
fi
@

LAB3's deadline is mid-January, so the first reminder goes out at the
end of January.
Checking \(\le 5\) covers January through May without needing a special
case for the year boundary.
<<add LAB3 if month is in range>>=
if [[ 10#$month -le 5 ]]; then
  modules+=("$MODULE_LAB3")
fi
@


\section{Fetching missing assignments}

[[missing_for_module]] wraps the [[canvaslms results --missing]] command
for a single module.
We pass [[-S $SUMMARY_STRATEGY]] because LAB3 contains a survey
(the diagnostic test), and without a custom summary module [[canvaslms]]
cannot compute a passing grade for it.
The same flag is harmless for KAL1 and LAB1, so we use it uniformly.

The output is tab-separated with five columns:
course, module, student e-mail, assignment name, motivation.
<<functions>>=
missing_for_module() {
  local module="$1"
  canvaslms results --missing \
    -c "$course" \
    -A "$module" \
    -S "$SUMMARY_STRATEGY"
}
@


\section{Sending reminders}

[[send_reminders]] collects all missing rows from Canvas across every
active module in a single pass, then sends one email per student
containing everything they are missing.

Collecting first and distributing second is critical for correctness:
if we queried Canvas per student we would get O(students) API calls, and
the data between calls could drift as Canvas updates in the background.
By collecting once we guarantee that each student's email reflects a
consistent snapshot.
<<functions>>=
send_reminders() {
  local all_missing
  all_missing=$(
    for module in "$@"; do
      missing_for_module "$module"
    done
  )

  if [ -z "$all_missing" ]; then
    return
  fi

  local students
  students=$(echo "$all_missing" | cut -f3 | sort -u)

  local subject
  subject=$(email_subject)

  for student in $students; do
    local body
    body=$(email_body "$student" "$all_missing")
    if [ -z "$body" ]; then
      continue
    fi
    if [ "${DRY_RUN:-0}" = "1" ]; then
      echo "Would send email to $student with subject '$subject' and body:"
      echo "$body"
      echo "----"
    else
      run_or_echo mutt -s "$subject" "$student" <<< "$body"
    fi
  done
}
@


\section{Composing the email}

\subsection{Subject}

The subject identifies the course and makes clear this is an automatic
reminder.
We omit the module name because the email may cover several modules at
once.
<<functions>>=
email_subject() {
  echo "[Påminnelse] ${course} --- du saknar betyg"
}
@

\subsection{Body}

[[email_body]] assembles a complete email body for one student.
It filters the full missing-rows snapshot down to that student's rows,
then iterates over each module present in those rows: printing the module
heading, its assignment lines, and any inline per-module instruction
immediately after.
General instructions that span multiple modules (the diagnostic test,
Restlabb) are collected at the end by [[how_to_proceed]].

Placing the KAL1 instruction immediately after the KAL1 assignments ---
rather than at the bottom with the other instructions --- means the
student sees the relevant venue information right next to the assignments
it applies to, without having to read past LAB1 or LAB3 entries first.

The student rows are passed as a string rather than re-querying Canvas,
which keeps the function pure and avoids a second network round-trip.
<<functions>>=
email_body() {
  local student="$1"
  local all_missing="$2"

  local student_rows
  student_rows=$(
    echo "$all_missing" | awk -F'\t' -v s="$student" '$3 == s'
  )

  echo "Hej!"
  echo ""
  echo "Du saknar fortfarande godkänt på följande uppgifter" \
       "i kursen ${course}:"
  echo ""
  local present_modules
  present_modules=$(echo "$student_rows" | cut -f2 | awk '!seen[$0]++')
  for module in $present_modules; do
    echo "${module}:"
    format_module_rows "$module" "$student_rows"
    echo ""
    module_instructions "$module"
  done
  how_to_proceed "$student_rows"
  echo ""
  echo "Med vänliga hälsningar,"
  echo "Daniel Bosk (automatisk påminnelse)"
}
@

\subsection{Formatting a single module's rows}

[[format_module_rows]] prints the assignment lines for one module,
indented under the heading that [[email_body]] has already printed.
Filtering to a single module here, rather than iterating over all modules
in one pass, keeps the function focused and makes [[email_body]]'s loop
easy to follow.
<<functions>>=
format_module_rows() {
  local module="$1"
  local rows="$2"
  echo "$rows" | awk -F'\t' -v mod="$module" \
    '$2 == mod { print "  - " $4 ": " $5 }'
}
@

\subsection{Per-module inline instructions}

Some modules need a short instruction placed immediately after their
assignment list, before the next module's entries.
KAL1 is the only such module: its assignments are presented at regular
lab sessions during the course, or at Lab Week after the course ends ---
not at the weekly Restlabb.
Putting this note right after the KAL1 list makes the connection between
assignments and venue explicit without forcing the student to scan to the
bottom of the email.

For all other modules [[module_instructions]] produces no output, so the
loop in [[email_body]] works uniformly regardless of how many modules are
active.
<<constants>>=
LAB_WEEK_URL="https://www.kth.se/social/group/labbvecka/"
@

<<functions>>=
module_instructions() {
  local module="$1"
  case "$module" in
    "$MODULE_KAL1")
      echo "KAL1-uppgifterna kan redovisas på de flesta labbtillfällen" \
           "under kursens gång. Efter kursens slut redovisas de under" \
           "Labbveckan:"
      echo "  ${LAB_WEEK_URL}"
      echo ""
      ;;
  esac
}
@

\subsection{How to proceed}

At the end of the email we emit instructions for assignments that are not
covered by the inline per-module notes above.
The diagnostic test is a special case: it is a Canvas quiz that students
complete entirely on their own, with no grader involvement, so the
instruction is simply to open Canvas and do it.
LAB1 and the project parts of LAB3 require presenting at a Restlabb
session.

We detect these two cases by scanning the student's rows once with
[[awk]] and setting flags.
Emitting each paragraph at most once avoids repetition when a student is
missing items in both LAB1 and LAB3.
KAL1 rows are excluded from [[needs_restlabb]] because their instructions
were already emitted inline by [[module_instructions]].
<<functions>>=
how_to_proceed() {
  local rows="$1"

  local needs_diagnostic needs_restlabb
  needs_diagnostic=$(
    echo "$rows" | awk -F'\t' \
      -v mod="$MODULE_LAB3" \
      -v diag="$DIAGNOSTIC_TEST" \
      '$2==mod && $4==diag {found=1} END {print (found ? 1 : 0)}'
  )
  needs_restlabb=$(
    echo "$rows" | awk -F'\t' \
      -v mod1="$MODULE_KAL1" \
      -v mod3="$MODULE_LAB3" \
      -v diag="$DIAGNOSTIC_TEST" \
      '$2!=mod1 && ($2!=mod3 || $4!=diag) {found=1} END {print (found ? 1 : 0)}'
  )

  if [ "$needs_diagnostic" = "1" ]; then
    echo "Det avslutande diagnostiska provet (\"${DIAGNOSTIC_TEST}\")" \
         "kan du göra när som helst direkt i Canvas --- du behöver inte" \
         "boka tid eller invänta rättning."
    echo ""
  fi
  if [ "$needs_restlabb" = "1" ]; then
    echo "Övriga uppgifter redovisas på Restlabb (drop-in, ingen" \
         "föranmälan krävs):"
    echo "  ${RESTLABB_URL}"
    echo ""
  fi
}
@

